// Info Wave W1 page 5/8 — "kenmore dryer not heating" (1,800/mo KD0). DIFFERENTIATOR:
// Kenmore = Sears house brand; first 3 model digits = actual maker. Verified mapping:
// 110/106=Whirlpool, 796/795=LG, 592=Samsung, 417/253=Frigidaire/Electrolux, 363=GE.
// Maker table in answer-first lead + hub cross-links to brand not-heating pages.
// Fire framing NFPA/USFA. gas vs electric. umbrella<->sniper links. $89.

import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../../../components/ServiceHero.astro';
import { MAIN_PHONE } from '../../../data/branches';
import { BRANCH_LOCATIONS_GEO_NEUTRAL } from '../../../lib/build-location-array';

const phone = MAIN_PHONE;
const phoneRaw = MAIN_PHONE.replace(/\D/g, '');
const phoneHref = `tel:+1${phoneRaw}`;
const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/dryer-repair/kenmore-not-heating/";

const title = "Kenmore Dryer Not Heating? Who Made Yours — Same Day";
const description = "Kenmore dryer won't heat? Kenmore is a Sears house brand — the first 3 digits of your model number reveal the real maker (Whirlpool, LG, Samsung), which decides the parts. Look it up, plus safe checks.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Who actually makes Kenmore dryers?",
    a: "Kenmore is a Sears house brand, not a manufacturer — the dryers are built by other companies and sold under the Kenmore name. The first three digits of your model number tell you who really built it: 110 (or 106) is Whirlpool, which made the majority of Kenmore laundry; 796 (or 795) is LG; 592 is Samsung; 417 or 253 is the Frigidaire/Electrolux family; and 363 is GE. This matters for a no-heat repair because a 110 Kenmore takes Whirlpool heating parts, a 796 takes LG parts, and a 592 takes Samsung parts — same badge, completely different hardware."
  },
  {
    q: "Why is my Kenmore dryer not heating?",
    a: "Whoever built it, the no-heat causes are the same family of parts. The leading cause is a restricted exhaust vent that makes the dryer overheat and blows a thermal fuse — the most common Kenmore no-heat part regardless of maker. After the fuse come the heating element and a thermostat or thermistor. On a gas Kenmore, a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor stops the burner. Clean the vent first; the specific replacement part depends on who made yours, which the model-number prefix tells you."
  },
  {
    q: "Where do I find my Kenmore model number?",
    a: "The model number is on a rating label, usually just inside the dryer door on the frame, on the back of the cabinet, or under the lid. It typically starts with three digits, a period or space, then more digits (for example 110.xxxxxxx). Those first three digits are the key: match them to the maker list above, and you'll know whether your no-heat repair needs Whirlpool, LG, Samsung, Frigidaire, or GE parts. If you're not sure, our techs identify the maker from the model number when you call."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Kenmore dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe, high-value step is the same no matter who made your Kenmore: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent — the hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap included. That resolves most no-heat calls and removes a real fire hazard. Also reset both halves of the 240V breaker on an electric Kenmore; if one leg trips, the drum tumbles but the heat is dead. Don't open the cabinet to test the fuse, element, or thermostat, and never touch the gas line or valve on a gas model. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire hazard?",
    a: "Yes — the clogged vent that most often stops a Kenmore from heating is also a leading fire cause. The U.S. Fire Administration reports roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. A Kenmore running hot or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day — it fixes the heat and removes the hazard together."
  }
];

