---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="dryer-repair/kenmore-not-heating"
  eyebrow="Kenmore Dryer Not Heating · Model Lookup Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Kenmore Dryer Won't Heat? First, Find Out Who Built It"
  subtitle="Kenmore is a Sears house brand — the first 3 digits of your model number reveal the real maker, and that decides which parts your no-heat repair needs. Look it up, then clear the vent (a fire risk) first."
  heroImageTitle="Kenmore Dryer Not Heating Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST + MAKER TABLE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · Start here</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Your Kenmore's model number tells you who really built it.</h2>

    <p><strong>Kenmore is a Sears house brand, not a manufacturer — the dryers are built by other companies and badged Kenmore. The first three digits of your model number identify the real maker, and that decides which heating parts your no-heat repair needs. Look up the prefix below. Whoever built it, the leading no-heat cause is the same: a clogged exhaust vent that overheats the dryer and blows its thermal fuse. Clean the vent first — it fixes most calls and removes a fire hazard. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <table class="maker-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Model starts with</th><th>Actually built by</th><th>Same diagnosis as</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>110</strong> or <strong>106</strong></td><td>Whirlpool <em>(most Kenmore laundry)</em></td><td><a href="/services/dryer-repair/whirlpool-not-heating/">Whirlpool not heating</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>796</strong> or <strong>795</strong></td><td>LG</td><td><a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-not-heating/">LG not heating</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>592</strong></td><td>Samsung</td><td><a href="/services/dryer-repair/samsung-not-heating/">Samsung not heating</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>417</strong> or <strong>253</strong></td><td>Frigidaire / Electrolux</td><td>generic <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>363</strong></td><td>GE</td><td><a href="/services/dryer-repair/ge-not-heating/">GE not heating</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your Kenmore from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. Clear it the same day regardless of who made your dryer.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHY IT MATTERS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Why the maker matters for a no-heat fix</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same badge, different parts.</h2>

    <p>Every Kenmore no-heat repair comes down to the same short list of parts — a thermal fuse, a heating element, a thermostat or thermistor, and on gas models an igniter, gas-valve coils, and a flame sensor. What changes with the maker is <em>which specific part</em> and its part number. A 110 Kenmore uses Whirlpool's thermal fuse and thermal cut-off kit; a 796 uses LG's fuse and element; a 592 uses Samsung's heating element and thermistor and can throw Samsung's HE overheat code. Ordering the right part the first time depends entirely on reading that model-number prefix — which is exactly why a generic "Kenmore dryer" parts guess so often gets it wrong.</p>

    <p>Find your model number on the rating label just inside the door, on the back of the cabinet, or under the lid. Match the first three digits to the table above, then follow the linked guide for your maker's specific diagnosis. Our techs do this identification for you when you call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES (universal) -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · The no-heat causes, whoever built it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">One short list of parts.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Restricted vent → blown thermal fuse (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes airflow, the dryer overheats, and a one-time thermal fuse trips and cuts the heat. It's the number-one no-heat part across every Kenmore maker, and it only stays fixed once the vent is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>The element heats the air; when it burns open, the dryer tumbles cold. The exact element differs by maker, but the symptom and the fix (test continuity, replace) are the same.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermostat / thermistor</h3>
      <p>A high-limit thermostat (Whirlpool-built) or a thermistor (LG/Samsung-built) can cut heat when it fails or misreads temperature. On Samsung-built Kenmores (592) this can show as an HE overheat code.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas Kenmore, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat calls — and removes the fire hazard — no matter who built your Kenmore.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Note your model prefix.</strong> Grab the first three digits so you (or we) can match the right maker and part.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix most Kenmore no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the fuse, element, or thermostat, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? We identify the maker and the part.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the Kenmore still won't heat, the fault is a failed part — and our techs identify the real maker from your model number, carry the fuse, element, and thermostat/thermistor that fit that platform, and replace only what failed. Reading the prefix means we bring the right Whirlpool, LG, or Samsung part the first time instead of guessing on a "Kenmore." <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide, the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference, and the maker-specific guides linked in the table above (<a href="/services/dryer-repair/whirlpool-not-heating/">Whirlpool</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/lg-not-heating/">LG</a>, <a href="/services/dryer-repair/samsung-not-heating/">Samsung</a>). A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/dryer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/dryer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Kenmore dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Kenmore dryer still cold after cleaning the vent? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

